{N.B. moves 1-14 are a reconstruction...} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5
Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Qc2 c6 8. Rd1 Re8 9. a3 h6 10. Bh4 Qa5 11. Bd3
dxc4 12. Bxc4 b5 13. Bd3 Bb7 14. O-O Rac8 {(JS note: the opening 14 moves not
given but there are some clues as to what some of them were. "The Pillsbury
Attack in the Q.G.D. was played and at move 7 Rhodes played the old move Qc2
to which Wahltuch replied with 7...c6. After Black's 14th move the following
position arose:")} 15. b4 Qc7 16. Bg3 Bd6 17. Bxd6 Qxd6 18. Ne4 Nxe4 19. Bxe4
Qb8 20. Qb2 Nf6 21. Bd3 Red8 22. Ne5 Nd5 23. Rc1 Qd6 24. Rc5 Nb6 25. Qe2 Nd7
26. Nxd7 Qxd7 27. Be4 Qe8 28. Rfc1 Kf8 29. Qf3 Rd6 30. Qg3 Qd7 31. Bc2 Rd5 32.
Bb3 Rxc5 33. Rxc5 Qc7 34. Qg4 a5 35. Bxe6 Re8 36. Bb3 axb4 37. axb4 Ra8 38. Rc1
{"and now Black exceeded the time limit. The reason for his doing so was that
in most tourna­ments the first session lasts two hours and to find the number
of moves required in the following session, when the time control operates
every hour, you usually add half the number of moves made in the first two
hours. Wahltuch unthinkingly applied this process and arrived at the figure 36
which he marked off on his score sheet as the point at which the time limit
would operate. Having reached that point and passed it he thought there was
plenty of time to spare, and was taking it easy when Rhodes claimed the game."
Source: CHESS, July 1945, Vol.10/118, p164} 1-0

